\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Below is a **clear, practical breakdown** of (A) the **cloud\f1\_\f0 enabled generator ecosystem** \f2\emdash  who the major manufacturers are, the typical cloud platforms, and what it takes to register and link them \emdash  and (B) how **local network protocols** (Modbus TCP, SNMP, BACnet, etc.) can be used as a **backup/secondary path** in case the cloud link is unavailable. I\rquote ll also include **how this fits into your FixMate backend setup**.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# **A. Cloud\f1\_\f0 Enabled Generator Manufacturers & Integration Paths**\par
\par
## **1. Major Cloud\f1\_\f0 Enabled Generator Brands**\par
\par
These are global generator OEMs whose industrial/commercial products often include cloud or remote monitoring capabilities (remote data, status, alerts):\par
\par
* **Caterpillar / Cat / FG Wilson (Caterpillar brand)** \f2\emdash  leading industrial gensets with remote monitoring (Cat Connect, VisionLink). ([Growth Market Reports][1])\par
* **Cummins** with **PowerCommand Cloud\f0\'99** remote monitoring for generators. ([Cummins Inc.][2])\par
* **Kohler / SDMO Industries** \f2\emdash  industrial generators with cloud support via Kohler systems. ([Growth Market Reports][1])\par
* **Generac Power Systems** (also offers remote data tools). ([Growth Market Reports][3])\par
* **MTU Onsite Energy**, **Atlas Copco**, **Himoinsa**, **Mitsubishi Heavy Industries** \emdash  industrial generators with remote telematics available through dealer platforms or partner cloud tools. ([Growth Market Reports][1])\par
\par
These manufacturers sell into **industrial and commercial markets** with telematics options; many dealer networks provide access to cloud platforms.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **2. Typical Generator Cloud Platforms**\par
\par
Each major manufacturer has one or more cloud/remote monitoring systems:\par
\par
| Manufacturer | Cloud Platform / Portal              | Typical Features                                                                                          |\par
| ------------ | ------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\par
| Caterpillar  | **Cat Connect / VisionLink**         | Real\f1\_\f0 time status, fault codes, fuel/runtime/fleet data \f2\emdash  requires subscription & dealer setup. ([Cat][4]) |\par
| Cummins      | **PowerCommand Cloud\f0\'99**              | Remote generator monitoring and control (mobile/web). ([Cummins Inc.][2])                                 |\par
| Kohler       | Kohler Cloud / proprietary portal    | Engine/alternator telemetry reporting                                                                     |\par
| Generac      | Generac Cloud / Connect Cloud        | Remote alerts & telemetry                                                                                 |\par
| Others       | OEM cloud apps via dealer interfaces | Typically tiered subscriptions                                                                            |\par
\par
**Note:** In some cases, OEM cloud access requires:\par
\par
* A subscription/service plan with the manufacturer or dealer.\par
* Registering the generator through the dealer or manufacturer portal.\par
* Credentials/authorization tokens specific to that account.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **3. Registering & Linking to Your FixMate Backend**\par
\par
Here\rquote s how this typically works in practice for a **cloud\f1\_\f0 enabled generator**:\par
\par
### **Step A \f2\emdash  User Registers Generator with Manufacturer**\par
\par
1. End user creates an account on the OEM\rquote s cloud portal (e.g., PowerCommand Cloud, Cat Connect portal).\par
2. User adds the generator to the portal (enter serial numbers and specs).\par
3. User ensures the generator has an active subscription and connectivity.\par
\par
**Important:** Most OEMs (e.g., Caterpillar) send **API credentials** (Client ID/Secret) tied to that user account and require verification with your (the customer\rquote s) or dealer\rquote s support team. ([digital.cat.com][5])\par
\par
### **Step B \emdash  Your FixMate App Collects Credentials**\par
\par
Users link their OEM account to FixMate by:\par
\par
* Entering **API key / client ID & secret**, or\par
* Completing an **OAuth flow** if the OEM supports it, so FixMate can fetch a **Bearer token**.\par
\par
### **Step C \emdash  Backend Polling Service**\par
\par
Your FixMate backend service:\par
\par
1. Uses the user\rquote s stored credentials to authenticate with the manufacturer\rquote s cloud API.\par
2. Polls telemetry endpoints at set intervals.\par
3. Normalizes this telemetry into the **FixMate standard schema**.\par
\par
Typical API interaction looks like:\par
\par
```\par
GET {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://api.oemcloud.com/v1/devices/\{generatorID\}/telemetry }}{\fldrslt{https://api.oemcloud.com/v1/devices/\{generatorID\}/telemetry\ul0\cf0}}}}\f2\fs22\par
Authorization: Bearer <user_oauth_token_or_api_key>\par
```\par
\par
### **Step D \emdash  Store & Present in FixMate**\par
\par
* Store the returned telemetry in your FixMate database.\par
* Drive dashboards, alerts, and rules engines with this data.\par
\par
**Key point:** You **must store and use each individual user\rquote s OEM credentials** rather than a single global key \emdash  the cloud platforms are scoped per user account. (See earlier answer on why a single FixMate key will not work for multiple customers.)\par
\par
---\par
\par
# **B. Local Network Telemetry as a Backup (Modbus, SNMP, BACnet)**\par
\par
Even if cloud integration is primary, you can use **local communication protocols** as a fallback or secondary telemetry path if cloud connectivity fails or is unavailable.\par
\par
## **1. Common Local Protocols Used in Generators**\par
\par
These protocols allow direct communication with the generator\rquote s controller over LAN or serial interfaces:\par
\par
| Protocol                        | Typical Usage                      | Notes                                                                                                                             |\par
| ------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\par
| **Modbus RTU / Modbus TCP**     | Engine data (runtime, load, temps) | Widely supported in industrial genset controllers; Modbus TCP works over LAN, Modbus RTU works over RS\f1\_\f0 485. ([Genesal Energy][6]) |\par
| **SNMP**                        | Status, alarms, device health      | Common in network\f1\_\f0 aware devices; can send traps on faults. ([BACnet International][7])                                            |\par
| **BACnet**                      | BMS/HVAC integration               | Standard for building automation (typically IP or MS/TP). ([BIG-EU][8])                                                           |\par
| **Others (e.g., OPC UA, MQTT)** | Used via gateways                  | For advanced integrations or cloud bridging. ([Beilai Tech. Co., Ltd.][9])                                                        |\par
\par
These can be used to pull telemetry locally \f2\emdash  with polling or event\f1\_\f0 based traps \f2\emdash  which you can ingest into FixMate when cloud fails.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## **2. How Local Protocol Backup Works**\par
\par
1. **Network Setup**: Connect the genset controller to LAN or serial network (RS\f1\_\f0 485/RS\f1\_\f0 232 \f3\u8594?\f0  LAN gateway if needed).\par
2. **Protocol Adapter**: Use a polling service (Node.js, Python) or an IoT gateway that speaks Modbus/SNMP/BACnet.\par
3. **Local Polling**: If cloud API is unreachable (due to internet outage or subscription issues), temporarily read telemetry locally.\par
4. **Sync to FixMate**: Save or cache data locally until cloud reconnects, then sync current state.\par
\par
**Example:** Polling Modbus TCP registers for runtime and temperatures every minute with a Node.js service. This service falls back to local reads if cloud calls fail.\par
*Local telemetry can act as a resilient backup source or primary source if cloud connectivity isn\rquote t reliable.*\par
\par
For industrial environments, often a **hybrid architecture** is used:\par
\par
* **Primary**: Cloud API for long\f1\_\f0 term storage, fleet tracking, and remote dashboards.\par
* **Secondary**: On\f1\_\f0 prem protocol reads for immediate alerts and redundancy.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# **C. Backend Architecture for FixMate**\par
\par
Your FixMate backend should include:\par
\par
## **1. Credential Management**\par
\par
* Store user\f1\_\f0 specific OEM credentials securely (e.g., encrypted).\par
* Refresh tokens/OAuth as required.\par
\par
## **2. Polling & Sync Layer**\par
\par
* **Cloud Adapter**: Poll cloud API for telemetry on schedule.\par
* **Local Adapter**: Poll local generator interface if cloud fails or as alternative.\par
\par
## **3. Normalization & Storage**\par
\par
* Map all telemetry (cloud or local) to a unified schema:\par
\par
  * `engineRuntimeHours`, `fuelLevelPercent`, `loadPercent`, etc.\par
\par
## **4. Triggers & Alerts**\par
\par
* Rule engine to surface:\par
\par
  * Fault codes\par
  * Low fuel\par
  * Overload / temperature thresholds\par
\par
## **5. Failover Logic**\par
\par
* If cloud API errors/timeouts:\par
\par
  * Attempt local protocol read\par
  * Log and switch automatically\par
  * Sync when cloud recovers\par
\par
